.TH CCCP 1 "April 16, 2026" "cccp 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cccp \- cloud code config provider; sync ~/.claude across machines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cccp
.I command
.RI [ flags ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cccp
synchronises the Claude Code state directory (by default
.IR $HOME/.claude )
across multiple hosts through a small HTTP + SQLite server.
Regular files are content-addressed and resolved last-write-wins;
.I *.jsonl
files are treated as append-only logs, with lines deduped across hosts by
content hash. Deletions propagate as tombstones.
.PP
One binary drives five subcommands:
.B serve
runs the server,
.B push
ships local state,
.B pull
downloads remote state,
.B watch
runs a sync daemon, and
.B explore
launches a standalone session viewer.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B serve
Run the storage server. Requires a bearer token (via
.BR --token ,
.BR CCCP_TOKEN ,
or
.BR config.token ).
When running, the server also hosts the session viewer at the
root URL behind cookie authentication.
.TP
.B push
Walk the allowlist, upload blobs and append log lines, then tombstone
paths that were previously pushed but have since disappeared from disk.
.IP
During push, the built-in secret scanner (cheka) examines each file for
API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys, connection strings, and other
sensitive material. All scanning is local; nothing leaves the machine if
secrets are found. By default, push fails on any finding. Use
.B --cheka-allow
to tolerate up to N findings, or
.B "--cheka-allow -1"
to disable the check. With
.BR --cheka-redact ,
secrets in log files are replaced with
.B [REDACTED:kind]
and the push proceeds; blob files with secrets still fail.
.TP
.B "pull [--full]"
Incremental pull: write only blob files whose content differs from local,
append log lines newer than the saved cursor (deduped against local).
With
.BR --full ,
rewrite every blob from the server snapshot and replay all log lines
from cursor=0. Useful for bootstrapping a fresh host.
.TP
.B watch
Run the sync daemon. Watches
.I ~/.claude
via fsnotify and pushes changes on a debounce window (default 500ms).
Subscribes to the server's SSE event stream and pulls on remote changes.
Can run standalone or be driven by Claude Code session hooks.
.IP
Sub-subcommands:
.RS
.TP
.B "watch hook-start"
Claude Code SessionStart hook. Reads JSON payload from stdin, records
the session id, and spawns the daemon if not already running.
.TP
.B "watch hook-end"
Claude Code SessionEnd hook. Reads JSON payload from stdin, drops
the session id. When no sessions remain, sends SIGTERM to the daemon.
.TP
.B "watch status"
Print daemon status: PID, session count, and liveness label. Liveness is
tri-state:
.B running
(pid exists and responds to signal 0),
.B "not running"
(no pidfile or the pid is gone), or
.B unknown
(pidfile is present but the process cannot be probed, typically because
it belongs to another UID).
.TP
.B "watch stop"
Send SIGTERM to the daemon.
.RE
.TP
.B "explore [dir]"
Launch the standalone session viewer. Reads Claude Code project
logs from the given directory (default
.IR $HOME/.claude/projects )
and serves a timeline UI on the configured port (default 9123).
.TP
.B host-id
Print this host's persistent UUID (generated on first run, stored under
.IR $dir/cccp/host_id ).
.TP
.B "help, -h, --help"
Print the built-in usage summary.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings resolve from four layers, later wins:
.IP "1." 4
Built-in defaults.
.IP "2."
JSON config file at
.I $dir/cccp/config.json
(override with
.B --config
or
.BR CCCP_CONFIG ).
.IP "3."
Environment variables.
.IP "4."
Command-line flags.
.PP
An explicit empty string in a higher layer still overrides a non-empty
lower layer \(em
.I false
and
.I unset
are not the same. For example,
.B --token ""
blanks out a token from
.I config.json
rather than falling through to it.
.PP
The config path itself is resolved in two passes: first,
.B $dir
is computed from defaults + env + CLI. The file at
.IR $dir/cccp/config.json
is then loaded. Finally, the full four-layer merge runs. A
.B dir
key inside
.I config.json
can redirect downstream paths (host_id, default DB) but cannot change
which config file was loaded.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
.BI "--agent " NAME
Agent profile:
.BR claude " (default), " codex ", " hermes ", " opencode .
Picks the default
.B --dir
(the agent's own state directory) and allowlist. Non-claude agents ship
every path under the wire prefix
.IR agent/NAME/ .
Env:
.BR CCCP_AGENT ;
config key
.BR agent .
.TP
.BI "--dir " PATH
Config root directory. Default: the agent's home,
.I $HOME/.claude
for claude.
Env:
.BR CCCP_DIR .
.TP
.BI "--url, --server-url " URL
Server base URL, e.g.
.IR https://host:12110 .
Env:
.BR CCCP_URL .
.TP
.BI "--token " TOKEN
Bearer token. Required by
.B serve
(to accept) and
.BR push / pull / watch
(to present). Env:
.BR CCCP_TOKEN .
.TP
.BI "--addr, --address " ADDR
Listen address for
.BR serve .
Default
.IR :12110 .
A value carrying an
.I http://
or
.I https://
scheme is treated as
.B --url
(with a warning) and the listen address resets to the default \(em a
listen address never has a scheme, so the user almost certainly meant
the server URL. Env:
.BR CCCP_ADDR .
.TP
.BI "--port " N
Listen port; replaces only the port portion of
.BR --addr .
Env:
.BR CCCP_PORT .
.TP
.BI "--db, --database, --db-path " PATH
SQLite database path. Default
.IR $dir/cccp/store.sqlite .
Env:
.BR CCCP_DB .
.TP
.BI "--config " PATH
Path to the JSON config file. Default
.IR $dir/cccp/config.json .
If specified explicitly and the file is missing, cccp exits with an error.
If the default path is missing, cccp continues silently. Env:
.BR CCCP_CONFIG .
.TP
.BI "--debounce-ms " N
Watch debounce window in milliseconds. Default 500.
Only meaningful for
.BR "cccp watch" .
Env:
.BR CCCP_DEBOUNCE_MS .
.TP
.BI "--cheka-allow " N
Tolerate up to N secret findings. Default 0 (fail on any).
Pass -1 to disable secret scanning entirely.
Applies to
.B push
and
.BR watch .
Env:
.BR CCCP_CHEKA_ALLOW .
.TP
.B --cheka-redact
Redact secrets in log files instead of blocking the push.
Each secret match is replaced with
.BR [REDACTED:kind] .
Blob files with secrets still fail (redacting would change the
content hash). Findings are logged to stderr as warnings.
Applies to
.B push
and
.BR watch .
.B cccp watch
defaults this to
.I true
unless some layer explicitly sets it to false \(em a background daemon
that silently stops syncing is worse than redacted log lines. Env:
.BR CCCP_CHEKA_REDACT .
.TP
.B --full
(\c
.B pull
only) Bootstrap: rewrite every blob, replay logs from cursor=0.
.TP
.BI "-p " PORT
(\c
.B explore
only) HTTP port for the session viewer. Default 9123.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CCCP_DIR
Alternate config root.
.TP
.B CCCP_URL
Server URL for
.BR push / pull / watch .
.TP
.B CCCP_TOKEN
Bearer token.
.TP
.B CCCP_ADDR
Listen address for
.BR serve .
.TP
.B CCCP_PORT
Listen port; overrides the port in
.BR CCCP_ADDR / --addr .
.TP
.B CCCP_DB
SQLite database path.
.TP
.B CCCP_CONFIG
Path to the JSON config file.
.TP
.B CCCP_DEBOUNCE_MS
Watch debounce window in milliseconds.
.TP
.B CCCP_CHEKA_ALLOW
Tolerate up to N secret findings (default 0; -1 disables).
.TP
.B CCCP_CHEKA_REDACT
Set to
.B true
to redact secrets in log files instead of blocking.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $dir/cccp/config.json
Optional JSON configuration. Recognised keys:
.BR dir ,
.BR url ,
.BR token ,
.BR addr ,
.BR port ,
.BR db ,
.BR debounce_ms ,
.BR cheka_allow ,
.BR cheka_redact ,
.BR allowlist
(array of strings).
.B allowlist
is the only knob that lives exclusively in the config file \(em it is
not available as a flag or environment variable.
Unknown keys cause a parse error.
.TP
.I $dir/cccp/host_id
Persistent host UUID, generated on first run.
.TP
.I $dir/cccp/state.json
Local sync bookkeeping: known pushed/pulled blob paths (deletion
tracking in both directions), the log cursor, per-log-file byte offsets
of lines already pushed, and a blob hash cache keyed on
.B (mtime_ns, size)
that lets unchanged files skip both hashing and secret scanning. Safe to
delete; will be rebuilt on next push/pull (at the cost of one full
re-hash and re-scan).
.TP
.I $dir/cccp/sessions.json, sessions.lock, secretary.pid, secretary.daemon.lock, secretary.log
Watch-daemon lifecycle state: the active Claude Code session list
(flocked through sessions.lock), the daemon's pidfile, the exclusive
lock held for the daemon's lifetime, and its log.
.TP
.I $dir/cccp/store.sqlite
Server-only SQLite database containing blobs, file versions, log lines,
and host records.
.SH ALLOWLIST
Entries ending in
.B /
are recursed; bare names are single files. Any
.I *.jsonl
file is routed to the append-only log pipeline regardless of its
position in the allowlist. If
.B allowlist
is omitted from
.IR config.json ,
the built-in default applies:
.PP
.RS
.IR agents/ ,
.IR commands/ ,
.IR file-history/ ,
.IR hooks/ ,
.IR memory/ ,
.IR paste-cache/ ,
.IR plans/ ,
.IR projects/ ,
.IR skills/ ,
.IR tasks/ ,
.IR CLAUDE.md ,
.IR history.jsonl ,
.IR settings.json .
.RE
.PP
Symlinks within allowlisted paths are resolved to their target content
and shipped under the symlink's path. Non-regular, non-symlink entries
(sockets, devices, pipes) are silently skipped.
.SH HTTP API
All endpoints except
.B /healthz
require
.BR "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" .
Write endpoints
.RB ( "POST /ingest" ", " "POST /log/append" )
additionally require an
.B X-Host-Id
header identifying the pushing host. The remaining endpoints
.RB ( "PUT /blob/{sha}" ", " "GET /current" ", " "GET /log/since" ", " "GET /events" )
will consult
.B X-Host-Id
when present (for echo suppression on
.BR /events )
but do not reject requests that omit it.
.TP
.B "POST /ingest"
Submit a file manifest as NDJSON. Each line is an object with fields
.BR path ,
.BR sha ,
.BR size ,
.BR mode ,
.BR mtime_ns .
A line with
.B sha=""
signals a deletion (tombstone). The server responds with a JSON object
containing
.B need
(list of SHAs it doesn't have yet),
.BR committed ,
.BR skipped ,
and
.B pending
counts. Ingest is two-phase: an entry whose blob is missing is
.I not
committed \(em it is counted as pending. The client must upload the
needed blobs and re-send the same manifest to commit them. An entry
whose latest stored version already matches is counted as skipped, so
re-pushing unchanged files appends no new versions.
.TP
.B "PUT /blob/{sha}"
Upload a content-addressed blob. The request body is the raw file
content; the server verifies its SHA256 matches the URL parameter.
Maximum size: 1 GiB. Returns 201 Created on success.
.TP
.B "GET /current"
Download the current LWW snapshot as a tar archive. Each entry is the
latest non-tombstoned version of a file, with mode and mtime preserved.
.TP
.B "POST /log/append"
Append log lines as NDJSON. Each line has fields
.BR path ,
.BR line
(the raw JSONL content), and
.BR suggested_time .
Lines are deduped by content hash per path. The response includes
.B new
and
.B dup
counts.
.TP
.B "GET /log/since?since={ns}"
Fetch log lines whose
.B first_seen
(server wall clock, nanoseconds) is strictly greater than the given
cursor. Returns NDJSON with fields
.BR path ,
.BR line ,
.BR suggested_time ,
.BR first_seen .
.TP
.B "GET /events"
Server-Sent Events stream. Broadcasts a JSON tick on every push,
containing the pushing host's ID.
.B cccp watch
subscribes to this for pull triggers. The connection is long-lived;
clients should reconnect with exponential backoff on failure.
.TP
.B "GET /healthz"
Returns
.B ok
with status 200. No authentication required.
.SH SECRET SCANNING
On every push, the built-in cheka scanner checks each file and log line
for sensitive material before any network calls. Patterns fall into two
tiers. Precise, service-specific shapes:
.PP
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Known token prefixes: Anthropic
.RB ( sk-ant- ),
OpenAI
.RB ( sk- ,
.BR sk-proj- ,
\&...), GitHub
.RB ( gh[pousr]_ ,
.BR github_pat_ ),
GitLab
.RB ( glpat- ),
Slack (tokens and webhook URLs), Stripe, Google (API keys and
.B ya29.
OAuth tokens), Hugging Face
.RB ( hf_ ),
npm, SendGrid, Docker Hub, DigitalOcean, Hasura, Synadia
.RB ( syt_ ).
.IP \(bu
JSON Web Tokens (three-segment base64url with eyJ headers).
.IP \(bu
AWS access keys (AKIA or temporary ASIA, followed by 16 uppercase
alphanumerics).
.IP \(bu
Private key block headers
.RB ( "-----BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY-----" ).
.RE
.PP
And generic shapes, which additionally pass through a denoise filter
(known documentation examples, placeholder markers, template literals,
environment-variable references, dictionary words, identifier-shaped
values, and low-entropy strings are all discarded):
.PP
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Connection strings with embedded passwords
.RB ( "://user:password@host" ).
.IP \(bu
Bearer tokens (20+ characters after "Bearer").
.IP \(bu
Key-value secrets
.RB ( PASSWORD= ", " TOKEN= ", " SECRET= ", " API_KEY= ,
etc., with values of 8+ characters).
.IP \(bu
Hex runs longer than 64 characters \(em git SHAs (40) and SHA256
digests (64) never trigger, and low-entropy dumps are filtered out.
.RE
.PP
Precise patterns run first and claim their span, so a JWT inside a
Bearer header reports once, under the most specific kind.
Binary files (non-UTF-8 content) are skipped. Findings are logged to
stderr with path, kind, and line number. By default, push fails if any
secrets are found. Use
.B "--cheka-allow N"
to tolerate up to N findings, or
.B "--cheka-allow -1"
to disable scanning entirely. With
.BR --cheka-redact ,
secrets found in log files are replaced in-flight with
.B [REDACTED:kind]
before the lines are sent to the server. Blob files with secrets still
block the push (redacting would change the content hash and cause
round-trip mismatches).
.SH SQLITE SCHEMA
The server stores all data in a single SQLite database with WAL mode,
NORMAL synchronous, and a single writer connection. The schema contains
four tables:
.TP
.B blobs
Content-addressed file storage. Primary key is the SHA256 hex digest.
.TP
.B file_versions
Append-only version history for each path. A version with
.B content_sha IS NULL
represents a deletion. The partial index
.B fv_content_sha
covers only non-NULL rows for efficient reverse lookups without
indexing tombstones.
.TP
.B hosts
Host registration with heartbeat timestamps.
.TP
.B log_lines
Append-only log storage. Primary key is
.BR "(path, line_hash)" ,
making dedup content-based rather than host-based. The
.B first_seen
column uses server wall-clock time; clients use it as their cursor for
incremental pulls. Never cursor by
.BR suggested_time .
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a server on port 12110 with a generated token:
.PP
.RS
.EX
export CCCP_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
cccp serve --port 12110
.EE
.RE
.PP
Bootstrap a client and ship local changes:
.PP
.RS
.EX
export CCCP_URL=https://host.example:12110 CCCP_TOKEN=...
cccp pull --full
cccp push
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run the watch daemon standalone:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cccp watch --url https://host.example:12110 --token "$CCCP_TOKEN"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Browse local sessions:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cccp explore
cccp explore -p 8080 /path/to/projects
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use a config file with a custom allowlist:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cat > ~/.claude/cccp/config.json <<EOF
{
  "url": "https://host.example:12110",
  "token": "my-secret-token",
  "allowlist": ["memory/", "hooks/", "CLAUDE.md"]
}
EOF
cccp push
.EE
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime error (network, disk, validation, secrets found).
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (unknown command or flag).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR curl (1),
.BR tar (1),
.BR systemd.service (5).
